    <!-- Question 15, Section 2.5/2.6 -->
    <exercise workspace="1in">
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Another application of linear systems is fitting polynomials.
                In fact, one can use linear algebra to do regressions and find
                the best-fitting polynomial for a given set of data points.
                You can learn how that works in MTH 205 (Linear Algebra II), or
                you can go ahead and try reading Section 6.5 of your textbook.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Suppose we have <m>(1,12), (2,9),</m> and <m>(3,5)</m> and
                    we want to find a quadratic polynomial that passes through
                    these points.
                    That means we are looking for a polynomial of the form
                    <m>p(x) = ax^2 + bx + c</m> where <m>a</m>, <m>b</m>, and
                    <m>c</m> are constants we want to find.
                    Using the point <m>(1,12)</m>, we can substitute
                    <m>x = 1</m> and <m>p(x) = 12</m> to get
                    <md>
                        12 = a (1)^2 + b(1) + c.
                    </md>
                    Use this strategy to find two other equations using the
                    points <m>(2,9)</m> and <m>(3,5)</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Explain why the first part gives you a linear system with
                    unknowns <m>a,b</m> and <m>c</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Solve the linear system (using Sage) to write the quadratic
                    that passes through these points.
                    Check that your polynomial works by using a graphing
                    software (like Desmos), or substituting the points to make
                    sure you get the correct output.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Consider the points <m>(-2,33), (1,6), (3,18), (4,15)</m>.
                    Use the method above to find a cubic (degree 3) polynomial
                    that passes through all 4 of these points.
                    Explain your work (don't just give a final answer, give a
                    solution!)
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Explain the meme.
                    Hint: Think about how many points you needed for a degree 2
                    polynomial and a degree 3 polynomial in the questions above.
                </p>
            </statement>

       
        </task>
    </exercise>
